{"name":"Shy Pink","hex":"#dfd9dc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tachawit. The native name for the Shawiya language."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily frightened; timid.","Reserved; disinclined to familiar approach."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shy"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shyness","blocks":[{"text":"Shyness is a psychological trait characterized by social inhibition and apprehension in response to new social environments or perceived social judgement. It is frequently associated with social avoidance. Although shyness can be an attribute of people who have low self-esteem, the trait is often defined by a concern regarding the negative evaluation of others, such as mocking or rejection. While shyness is considered a normal personality variant, it is distinct from clinical conditions such as social anxiety disorder, which involves impairment in daily functioning."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/20220801_Introversion_-_Shyness_-_Social_anxiety_disorder_-_comparative_chart.svg/330px-20220801_Introversion_-_Shyness_-_Social_anxiety_disorder_-_comparative_chart.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shyness"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to 